[{:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "q-109094"] :question/problem "Find the Jordan canonical form of the matrix \n$A=[\\begin{aligned}-8 & 5 \\\\ -5 & 2\\end{aligned}].$" :question/worked-solution "Every $n\\times n$ matrix $A$ can be written in the form\n\n$$\nA=PJ{P}^{-1},\n$$\n\nwhere $J$ is a Jordan canonical form (or Jordan normal form) of $A,$ and $P$ is an invertible matrix.\n\nTo find the matrix $J,$ we first find the eigenvalues of the matrix $A$ by solving $det(A-\\lambda I)=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\begin{aligned}-8-\\lambda  & 5 \\\\ -5 & 2-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (-8-\\lambda )(2-\\lambda )-5\\cdot (-5) & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}+6\\lambda +9 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda +3)(\\lambda +3) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the eigenvalues are ${\\lambda }_{1}={\\lambda }_{2}=-3.$ \n\nSince $A$ is not a scalar matrix, we conclude that $A$ is not diagonalizable, and its Jordan canonical form is\n\n$$\nJ={J}_{2}(-3)=[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -3\\end{aligned}].\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "q-109314/selection/c369935bde46c0fb6ea7a5427f202e440cc79c01bdf660719682f9b1c70f3db8" :answer/id #uuid "c156b260-b97a-520d-af07-01844b538105" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-109314/selection/9e02ac843816750d483fa24d0bcae89d715456cc4932772f1422cbab38002b73" :answer/id #uuid "ad0cfe53-af26-555e-8e16-9106902f2806" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-109314/selection/ab1aefe114382a4b3ee89b9806b4611a607d67fcbef79dd523ff05acfcfee5ae" :answer/id #uuid "a9abccb2-ccf2-5651-8df0-c4163784ed67" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-109314/selection/85ae5fb30e4d0be5d4b04e5b301e923092c184404fabee159f16a22a8cbb1cc6" :answer/id #uuid "4d7fdc8f-2c8a-5247-ac2f-c5a920323e43" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-109314/selection/658868198dba8b7c0d297496665e9d1cf915bcb0f5613ed3aabc5d835098db81" :answer/id #uuid "8667fc82-527b-5beb-9ae7-624429a536a7" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-109314/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "f17853dc-60a5-5d04-ac99-da8cf84207e9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-109314/selection/c369935bde46c0fb6ea7a5427f202e440cc79c01bdf660719682f9b1c70f3db8" :answer-field/choices ["q-109314/selection/c369935bde46c0fb6ea7a5427f202e440cc79c01bdf660719682f9b1c70f3db8" "q-109314/selection/9e02ac843816750d483fa24d0bcae89d715456cc4932772f1422cbab38002b73" "q-109314/selection/ab1aefe114382a4b3ee89b9806b4611a607d67fcbef79dd523ff05acfcfee5ae" "q-109314/selection/85ae5fb30e4d0be5d4b04e5b301e923092c184404fabee159f16a22a8cbb1cc6" "q-109314/selection/658868198dba8b7c0d297496665e9d1cf915bcb0f5613ed3aabc5d835098db81"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-109314" :question/id #uuid "d7242a7b-99e3-5e08-a5ad-d2a9535cb71f" :question/math-academy-id "q-109314" :question/problem "Find a Jordan canonical form of the matrix \n$A=[\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{aligned}].$" :question/worked-solution "Every $n\\times n$ matrix $A$ can be written in the form\n\n$$\nA=PJ{P}^{-1},\n$$\n\nwhere $J$ is a Jordan canonical form (or Jordan normal form) of $A,$ and $P$ is an invertible matrix.\n\nTo find the matrix $J,$ we first find the eigenvalues of the matrix $A$ by solving $det(A-\\lambda I)=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\begin{aligned}-\\lambda  & -1 \\\\ 2 & 3-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (-\\lambda )(3-\\lambda )-(-1)(2) & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}-3\\lambda +2 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda -2)(\\lambda -1) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSo, the eigenvalues are ${\\lambda }_{1}=1$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=2.$ Since we get two distinct eigenvalues, the matrix $A$ is diagonalizable, and a Jordan canonical form is\n\n$$\nJ={J}_{1}(1)\\,⊕\\,{J}_{1}(2)=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-109314/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "65f20482-73ea-5f1c-bb2b-1d7472fbfd4e"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-109314"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-112445/selection/fa7f3e5c59e8f950dab4226f14caabd9409914dea32e1f84540d4aa3369c5a93" :answer/id #uuid "86943ba7-813e-51a7-af26-a1ee71efae96" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-6 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -6\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-112445/selection/da02c4538215ef5cefc3b76645a0eaebedb5e8aad2a46fbe55c3aa3319767e4e" :answer/id #uuid "3c3f9774-b1eb-5f28-bca1-70683bb2da8b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-6 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-112445/selection/029818d9673984398de9799c0d44f860df98bb34258636b798273940369d2d21" :answer/id #uuid "21873374-4e4d-593e-939e-c022136782fc" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-6 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -6\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-112445/selection/6d842213cf436eade8d1975cdf4e2906555811ed6f58844aa22fcf4f97c43d77" :answer/id #uuid "1fa31d06-0760-5f04-a559-07b3316aec96" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & -6 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-112445/selection/116ba511f78f3ed888236c80aa0db20008860383f2b47eb517f411d99b4bfe21" :answer/id #uuid "db05d747-1713-5089-abec-212af06894b6" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-112445/selecti