$ is a Jordan canonical form (or Jordan normal form) of $A,$ and $P$ is an invertible matrix. \n\nNotice that $A$ is a scalar matrix, which means that it is already in Jordan canonical form. Therefore, it has a single (repeated) eigenvalue $\\lambda =-5,$ and\n\n$$\nJ=A=-5I=[\\begin{aligned}-5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -5\\end{aligned}].\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "q-112475/selection/5de4f401a57027a3e600c8dbaa6038518a62e6ff86cc1c4c6d2a3d0084a30d3e" :answer/id #uuid "80e79a9d-a2f9-58ab-a2bf-3af3172c71d1" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-112475/selection/7f41b6486ad648ff8ea6f92e114bd03c729de757d02f0cb5c3ecb2b30475730c" :answer/id #uuid "e2d225bd-1e31-54d7-9fb8-69a5096f30be" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-112475/selection/4eebab2f3b261b452fe5dc6257904d0416de47fbf0476b310a5ddc8f4c54732c" :answer/id #uuid "c21b78a8-2fa7-5746-a5be-bbd5eb9b35c3" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-4 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -4\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-112475/selection/ece97ca0208b5d8a2a994c38819b780fb7feacc85f97f8c14e6230ce8bea75b2" :answer/id #uuid "3447641e-8656-501b-8ab8-d20cec284b76" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -4\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-112475/selection/6654b1b8d59c966b7fb7708d4f2280a54bfa59f0b15193edc8b35c866afb2d49" :answer/id #uuid "605d34ec-e855-54ce-a2fc-8011c0a1a54e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-4 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-112475/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "ba42c2a0-015b-5e3d-b56e-c1d499e98dbf" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-112475/selection/4eebab2f3b261b452fe5dc6257904d0416de47fbf0476b310a5ddc8f4c54732c" :answer-field/choices ["q-112475/selection/5de4f401a57027a3e600c8dbaa6038518a62e6ff86cc1c4c6d2a3d0084a30d3e" "q-112475/selection/7f41b6486ad648ff8ea6f92e114bd03c729de757d02f0cb5c3ecb2b30475730c" "q-112475/selection/4eebab2f3b261b452fe5dc6257904d0416de47fbf0476b310a5ddc8f4c54732c" "q-112475/selection/ece97ca0208b5d8a2a994c38819b780fb7feacc85f97f8c14e6230ce8bea75b2" "q-112475/selection/6654b1b8d59c966b7fb7708d4f2280a54bfa59f0b15193edc8b35c866afb2d49"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-112475" :question/id #uuid "86ca96fe-ce68-51c6-b6c2-48af3e332f5f" :question/math-academy-id "q-112475" :question/problem "Find the Jordan canonical form of the matrix \n$A=[\\begin{aligned}-6 & -1 \\\\ 4 & -2\\end{aligned}].$" :question/worked-solution "Every $n\\times n$ matrix $A$ can be written in the form\n\n$$\nA=PJ{P}^{-1},\n$$\n\nwhere $J$ is a Jordan canonical form (or Jordan normal form) of $A,$ and $P$ is an invertible matrix.\n\nTo find the matrix $J,$ we first find the eigenvalues of the matrix $A$ by solving $det(A-\\lambda I)=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\begin{aligned}-6-\\lambda  & -1 \\\\ 4 & -2-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (-6-\\lambda )(-2-\\lambda )-(-1)\\cdot (4) & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}+8\\lambda +16 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda +4)(\\lambda +4) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the eigenvalues are ${\\lambda }_{1}={\\lambda }_{2}=-4.$ \n\nSince $A$ is not a scalar matrix, we conclude that $A$ is not diagonalizable, and its Jordan canonical form is\n\n$$\nJ={J}_{2}(-4)=[\\begin{aligned}-4 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -4\\end{aligned}].\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-112475/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b629bead-e64a-5e73-ae42-80d4ebb3762b"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-112475"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]}]
